[FINAL-TEXT] r1 (router-id 10.0.0.76) is **absent from csw1's OSPF neighbors**. csw1 has no default route. Case closed.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r1
diagnosis_text:
The FRR routing package is entirely absent from router r1. The device has a valid FRR configuration file at `/etc/frr/frr.conf` — correctly specifying OSPF, advertising its uplink subnets (10.0.0.32/30 and 10.0.0.36/30 toward csw1/csw2), and crucially containing `default-information originate` — but the FRR binaries themselves are not installed. No zebra/ospfd process exists anywhere on the system, there is no frr runit service entry, and `systemctl status frr` returns "Unit not found."

Without FRR running, r1 never joins the campus OSPF domain. Confirmed directly: csw1's OSPF neighbour table lists five adjacencies (csw2, dswa1, dswa2, dswb1, dswb2) but r1's router-id 10.0.0.76 is completely absent. As a result, r1's default route to ISP A (203.0.113.1/eth2) is never redistributed into OSPF. Every downstream device — csw1, csw2, dswa1, dswa2 — has no default route. Internet-bound packets from PC1 (10.1.0.100) reach the VRRP gateway dswa1 (10.1.0.2), which has no forwarding path, and dswa1 immediately returns ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable." TCP connections from the user's browser hang until their own timeout before failing, producing the "treacle slow with periodic hangs" experience the user described. The iptables MASQUERADE rules on r1 (covering 10.1.0.0/24 through 10.6.0.0/24) are correct and will work once traffic can actually reach r1.

Root cause: FRR is not installed on r1, so the OSPF daemon cannot start and no default route is propagated into the campus routing domain.

Corrective fix: Install the FRR package on r1 (`apt-get install frr`), enable the ospfd daemon in `/etc/frr/daemons`, and start the service. Once r1 establishes OSPF adjacencies with csw1 and csw2 and advertises the default route, all internal devices will immediately learn it via OSPF and internet connectivity will be restored.
=== END ===